## Why This Is Asked
Designing a versioning layer tests understanding of prompt drift, fallback logic, and observability in prompts.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned prompts, fallback strategies
- Signals: intent, confidence, version_stability
- Edge cases: outdated version, conflicting signals

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal version routing prototype
function routePrompt(prompt, version, scores) {
  const versions = ['v1','v2','v3'];
  let chosen = version && versions.includes(version) ? version : versions
    .map((v,i)=>({v, s: (scores && scores[i]) || 0}))
    .sort((a,b)=> b.s - a.s)
    [0].v;

  // Fallback logic for edge cases
  if (scores[chosen] < threshold || isOutdated(chosen)) {
    chosen = findMostStableVersion(versions, chosen);
    logFallback(chosen, prompt, scores);
  }

  return {
    version: chosen,
    plan: generatePlan(prompt, chosen),
    payload: buildPayload(prompt, chosen)
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design modular, policy-driven prompt systems that scale across tenants, balance latency with safety, and provide auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Modular prompt blocks
- Policy graph
- Per-tenant constraints
- Latency-aware composition
- Determinism and reproducibility
- Provenance and audit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch (pseudo-implementation)
function composePrompt(intent, blockLibrary, policy){
  const allowed = blockLibrary.filter(b => policy.allows(b.tag, intent));
  const prompt = allowed.map(b => b.template).join("\n");
  const audit = { intent, blocks: allowed.map(b => b.id), provenance: policy.provenance };
  return { prompt, audit };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate determinism when blocks or transformations are nondeterministic?
- How would policy graph evolution affect audit integrity and prompt reproducibility?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, privacy-aware prompt routing with safety checks under latency constraints, a common challenge in enterprise LLM deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware routing
- Privacy-preserving sanitization
- Latency-budget guided model selection
- Guardrails and veto logic
- Pluggable sanitizer architecture

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal gating prototype (JS)
class Gate {
  constructor() {}
  route(prompt, tenant) {
    const risk = {
      privacy: tenant.privacyRisk || 0,
      safety: tenant.safetyRisk || 0,
      latency: tenant.latencyBudget || 100
    };
    const model = risk.latency < 60 ? 'lite' : 'standard';
    const template = risk.privacy > 7 || risk.safety > 7 ? 'guarded' : 'default';
    return { tenant: tenant.id, model, template };
  }
}
const g = new Gate();
console.log(g.route('Prompt', {id: 'tenantA', privacyRisk:8, safetyRisk:5, latencyBudget:40}));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guardrails under adversarial prompts?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic policies?

## Why This Is Asked

